1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,980
Agents are buying from your store.

2
00:00:04,030 --> 00:00:07,929
When one goes wrong, who's at fault?

3
00:00:07,979 --> 00:00:14,511
Undo answers that, and settles it through PayPal in minutes.

4
00:00:14,561 --> 00:00:17,802
Here my agent shops for a tee.

5
00:00:17,852 --> 00:00:21,642
I approve the exact item on PayPal,

6
00:00:21,692 --> 00:00:25,152
and Undo stores what I approved,

7
00:00:25,202 --> 00:00:29,650
hashed into the PayPal order's custom ID.

8
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:32,140
A navy tee shows up.

9
00:00:32,190 --> 00:00:35,164
The buyer's agent opens a claim,

10
00:00:35,214 --> 00:00:39,606
and Undo asks for a photo with a one-time code.

11
00:00:39,656 --> 00:00:45,276
The vision model reads the color, the size tag and the code.

12
00:00:45,326 --> 00:00:49,907
A deterministic fault engine compares it with the

13
00:00:49,957 --> 00:00:53,593
PayPal order: the merchant is at fault.

14
00:00:53,643 --> 00:00:57,184
The store's own rules make the offers.

15
00:00:57,234 --> 00:01:02,760
The agent picks the keep-it refund, and PayPal confirms it.

16
00:01:02,810 --> 00:01:04,650
Both screens update.

17
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:07,476
Now a delegated purchase.

18
00:01:07,526 --> 00:01:12,285
The person approved a spending limit for a size M tee,

19
00:01:12,335 --> 00:01:14,689
and the agent picked later.

20
00:01:14,739 --> 00:01:16,292
It bought a large.

21
00:01:16,342 --> 00:01:21,457
The person didn't approve that, and neither did the store.

22
00:01:21,507 --> 00:01:23,684
So the agent is at fault,

23
00:01:23,734 --> 00:01:29,650
and the agent platform pays the buyer back, through PayPal Payouts.

24
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:34,128
"My agent bought this without asking me."

25
00:01:34,178 --> 00:01:38,856
But the person approved this exact cart on PayPal,

26
00:01:38,906 --> 00:01:43,679
the hash still matches, and the right item arrived.

27
00:01:43,729 --> 00:01:47,557
So the claim is denied, with the reasons,

28
00:01:47,607 --> 00:01:51,151
and with a link to escalate to PayPal,

29
00:01:51,201 --> 00:01:54,650
which always stays the final arbiter.

30
00:01:55,300 --> 00:01:58,947
A hundred-and-twenty-dollar jacket.

31
00:01:58,997 --> 00:02:04,862
The merchant asks the AI assistant to refund it in full.

32
00:02:04,912 --> 00:02:07,926
The assistant can only draft.

33
00:02:07,976 --> 00:02:12,785
The policy evaluator blocks the draft: keep-it

34
00:02:12,835 --> 00:02:16,693
refunds are capped at thirty percent.

35
00:02:16,743 --> 00:02:24,298
The thirty-six dollar refund the rules allow goes to the approval queue,

36
00:02:24,348 --> 00:02:26,411
and one tap pays it.

37
00:02:26,461 --> 00:02:29,791
No AI ever touches a money tool,

38
00:02:29,841 --> 00:02:34,650
and every step is on a hash-chained audit log.

39
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:37,882
If the buyer goes to PayPal anyway,

40
00:02:37,932 --> 00:02:41,416
Undo stops making offers and answers in PayPal.

41
00:02:41,466 --> 00:02:44,649
Here the store was at fault, so it accepts,

42
00:02:44,699 --> 00:02:46,679
and PayPal closes the case.

43
00:02:46,729 --> 00:02:51,417
Thirteen scenarios, measured in the sandbox: every fault right,

44
00:02:51,467 --> 00:02:54,650
every remedy right, zero policy violations.
